A||||3||theogre||Critical: Brake Upgrade? Service? UPDATE||||4||
Z||000000||theogre||03-01-2001||08:28 PM||||I've posted the nearly finished brake upgrade article, tittled "Stop That!," to the Body & Chassis section of the Fiero Cave. (I will be verifying a few specifics as soon as the dead MC someone is sending me gets here. Overall that information won't change the article much. It will just fill in a few details on the stock MC that I can't get any other way.)<P>The article covers questions and issues from various threads here and raises new posibilities as well.<P>This article is a must read for anyone doing major work on Fiero brakes. Especially if you are considering the "Grand Am Upgrade."<P>Read this article even if you have already done the grand am "upgrade." There is new information that is important to getting the system to work right. Especially if you have done the front axle only.<P>IF you've done the 4 wheel upgrade and hate it there is info for you as well.<P>The article also gives a basic explanation of just how the stock master cylinder works. It is important to under stand this item and how it is setup in Fiero Before Any upgrade is attempted.<P>------------------<BR><I><B>Screamin Yellow Zonkers... If it's Screamin and Yellow, I ain't eatin it.</B></I>||||reg||4
Z||000001||87GTZ34||03-02-2001||09:46 AM||||Cool!  Thanks Ogre for your knowledge, time and effort! I'll defer reading to my time as I don't want to read it on the clock.<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 03-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tryxalon||03-02-2001||09:13 PM||||Wow!! Fantastic stuff Ogre!! Great research, great logic, and fantastic explination!! <P>Thanks!! ||||reg||4
Z||000003||theogre||03-02-2001||11:07 PM||||The current version is a semi final draft of it. There are some changes already. I should have the rest together next week. (I'm waiting on a dead MC someone is sending me.) A few people asked so I wanted to get the bulk of it online. I've already found out I need to rework the explanation of a couple things. <P>I'm also looking to adress the machine work issues of using the GA rotors up front. I have to talk to some people about a slightly differant rotor. The new rotor may raise the price but will reduce mechanical issues a huge amount. One thing to be determined is how much, if any, the wheel mounting surface of the Fiero hub can be cut back to provide a centering collar for the new rotor and to reduce the distance the wheel is pushed out. (The affected area of the hub is tapered internally. cut to much in the wrong place and the hub can break.)<P>Someone mentioned wheel centering in another thread... I'm not as worried about wheel center index. After market wheels are commonly centered just by the lugs without problem. I don't see any big reason there should be a problem with OE wheels doing the same thing.<BR>||||reg||4
